# Corpus item 3: a resource function showing read / edit / take, with no
# use-after-move.
#
# Status: executable-today, on the bounded record frontend.
# Run: cc -std=c11 -O2 bootstrap/stage2/record_frontend.c -o FRONTEND
#      FRONTEND tests/usability/03_ownership_modes.kofun OUT.ir OUT.layout OUT.run
#      # OUT.run records: call|function=lifecycle|result=14
#
# Two of the three modes run. The third does not exist, and that is a
# decision rather than missing work.
#
#   read  — a borrowed view. `handle_of` below is called twice on the same
#           binding and neither call consumes it.
#   take  — a move. `release` consumes the session; the caller cannot use it
#           again, and the checker proves that rather than trusting a naming
#           convention.
#   edit  — refused. `fn shift(edit token: Token)` is rejected with
#           "error[E2S121]: `edit` access to a record is unsupported in v1;
#           record fields are immutable"
#           (tests/conformance/records/edit_parameter.kofun). This is DD-021:
#           records are immutable, whole-value, and #909 does not reopen it.
#           tests/usability/check.sh asserts that refusal so the row cannot
#           quietly become wrong if the boundary moves.
#
# The mode keywords are frontend-specific, which is the finding rubric.md
# scores under "semantic surprises". `read` and `take` parameters are
# understood by the bounded record frontend
# (bootstrap/stage2/record_frontend.c) and by nothing else. Run this same
# file through `./bin/kofun check` and it is refused — here on the `Text`
# field, which Stage 2 reaches first: "record `Session` has a field type
# outside the Stage 2 Int/Bool/Text slice" (E2S32). Remove the `Text` field and
# Stage 2 gets one step further and then reports "unknown lexical binding
# `session`", because it has parsed `read session: Session` as a parameter
# named `read` and never bound `session` at all. The word that makes
# ownership explicit is not merely unsupported on the production path; it is
# silently reinterpreted as an identifier. check.sh pins both refusals.
#
# There is no use-after-move here, by construction. The refusal that proves
# the checker would catch one is already checked in at
# tests/conformance/records/use_after_move.kofun, which reports
# "error[E2S123]: `token` was moved by `take` and cannot be used again".
# This corpus references that fixture rather than copying it; check.sh runs
# it.

type Session = {
    handle: Int,
    label: Text,
}

# read: a borrowed view. The caller keeps the session.
fn handle_of(read session: Session) -> Int {
    return session.handle
}

# take: a move. The session is consumed and cannot be used again.
fn release(take session: Session) -> Int {
    take session
    return 0
}

# Two reads of one binding, neither of which consumes it.
fn lifecycle() -> Int {
    let session = Session(handle: 7, label: "primary")
    let first = handle_of(session)
    let second = handle_of(session)
    return first + second
}
